{"format":"hologram.registry.artifact/v1","id":"mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/redis","fetched":"2026-09-25T12:12:11.262Z","source":{"name":"Microsoft Artifact Registry","api":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/api/v1/catalog/cbl-mariner/base/redis/details?reg=mar","page":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/en-us/product/cbl-mariner/base/redis/about"},"readme":"***Warning: Azure Linux 2.0 will reach its End of Life (EOL) on July 31st, 2025. After this date, it will no longer receive updates, security patches, or support, which could leave your systems vulnerable. We strongly recommend upgrading to an Azure Linux 3.0 image before July 31st, 2025.***\n\n## About\n\nThis repository contains the official Redis image for CBL-Mariner.\n\n### What is CBL-Mariner?\n\nCBL-Mariner is an internal Linux distribution for Microsoft’s cloud infrastructure and edge products and services. CBL-Mariner is designed to provide a consistent platform for these devices and services and will enhance Microsoft’s ability to stay current on Linux updates.  \n\nTo learn more about CBL-Mariner, refer to our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n### What is Redis?\nRedis is an open-source, networked, in-memory, key-value data store with optional durability. It is written in ANSI C. The development of Redis is sponsored by Redis Labs today; before that, it was sponsored by Pivotal and VMware. According to the monthly ranking by DB-Engines.com, Redis is the most popular key-value store. The name Redis means REmote DIctionary Server.\n\nSee: [Redis](https://redis.io/)\n\n## Featured tags\n\n- `6`\n- `6-cm2.0`\n\n## Usage\n\n### Security\nRedis, as a default configuration, comes with protected mode enabled. However, the Redis containers accessible on Docker Hub diverge from this default setting – in these containers, protected mode is disabled by default for ease of use. For optimal security, it is **strongly** advised to enable protected mode and establish a password (by providing a configuration file) when intending to expose your Redis instance to the internet. Refer to the following links for more information on security configurations for Redis:\n\n* [Redis documentation on security](https://redis.io/topics/security)\n* [Protected mode](https://redis.io/topics/security#protected-mode)\n* [A few things about Redis security by antirez](http://antirez.com/news/96)\n\n### How to use the image\n\nGenerally, you need to have a docker container that runs Redis server and then have Redis client that talks to it. By default, the container ships with both the server and client to be available, so you can run second container with the client that is connected to the server for interfacing, alternatively you can use other means to interface with it.\n\n#### Start a Redis server instance\n\nTo start a Redis server instance, create a dedicated network for both the Redis server and redis-cli using the command `docker network create my-redis-network`. Subsequently, ensure connectivity between both containers by utilizing the `--network my-redis-network` docker parameter. After you have network created/configured, you can start the server using the command:\n\n\tdocker run --name my-redis-server --network my-redis-network -d mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/redis:6 redis-server --protected-mode no\n\n**Note:** For convenience, the above command initializes the Redis server with protected mode turned off, making it suitable for development and testing purposes. However, it is **strongly** advised to have protected mode turned on and establish a password (via a config file) if you intend to expose your Redis instance to the internet for enhanced security. Refer to the section [Security](#security) for more details.\n\n#### Connecting via redis-cli\n\nTo start redis-cli container in interactive mode and connect it to the server container which was started from section [Start a Redis Server Instance](#start-a-redis-server-instance) run the following command:\n\n\tdocker run -it --network my-redis-network --rm mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/redis:6 redis-cli -h my-redis-server\n\nIf you want to run a simple command in redis-cli to skip the interactive mode, you can always pass the command like this:\n\n\tdocker run -it --network my-redis-network --rm mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/redis:6 redis-cli -h my-redis-server LPUSH mylist a b c d\n\n#### Redis configuration\nBy default, the image is shipped with default `redis.conf` file which is located in `/etc/redis.conf` path. When no arguments are provided to the Redis server, it will use the `redis.conf` file as configuration. However, when any argument is provided, the `redis.conf` file will not be used as the default configuration file which will be indicated by a log message within the server container. You can always provide path for a config file you want the server to use, an example of such a command is:\n\n\tdocker run --name my-redis-server --network my-redis-network -d mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/redis:6 redis-server /path/to/config/container.conf\n\nAlternatively, you can craft your custom image based on the Mariner Redis image, incorporating your specific configuration file:\n\n```\nFROM mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/redis:6\nCOPY redis.conf /etc/redis/redis.conf\nCMD [ \"redis-server\", \"/etc/redis/redis.conf\" ]\n```\n\n### Launch Mariner Shell\n\nThe Redis image ships with a bash shell. The following command will launch the shell:\n\n\tdocker run -it mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/redis:6 /bin/bash\n\n## Support\n\nCBL-Mariner container images are supported only for internal Microsoft use. For more information please see our [Support Statement](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/SUPPORT.md).\n\n## License\n\nThe CBL-Mariner distribution is provided under the [MIT license](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/LICENSE). Individual packages and components are governed by their own licenses included in the package sources available via srpm on [packages.microsoft.com](https://packages.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/) or via our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n- Legal Notice: [Container License Information](https://aka.ms/mcr/osslegalnotice)","readmeBase":null,"publisher":{"name":"Microsoft","url":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/","official":true,"verified":true},"registered":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T16:54:49.9517939+00:00","categories":[],"keywords":[],"license":"MIT","links":[],"maintainers":[],"stats":{"pulls":null,"pullsText":"21M+","stars":null},"mediaTypes":[],"contentTypes":[],"storage":null,"tags":[{"name":"6.2-cm2.0","digest":"sha256:607463becc60b14515e5370a7d2c7c68c4dbadb51b086a318abbbef2395c00be","pushed":"2026-03-12T21:31:09.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"6.2","digest":"sha256:607463becc60b14515e5370a7d2c7c68c4dbadb51b086a318abbbef2395c00be","pushed":"2026-03-12T21:30:59.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"6-cm2.0","digest":"sha256:607463becc60b14515e5370a7d2c7c68c4dbadb51b086a318abbbef2395c00be","pushed":"2026-03-12T21:30:49.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"6","digest":"sha256:607463becc60b14515e5370a7d2c7c68c4dbadb51b086a318abbbef2395c00be","pushed":"2026-03-12T21:30:38.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"6.2.20-1-cm2.0.20260311","digest":"sha256:607463becc60b14515e5370a7d2c7c68c4dbadb51b086a318abbbef2395c00be","pushed":"2026-03-12T21:30:30.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"6.2.20-1-cm2.0.20260311-arm64","digest":"sha256:74dcb